until recently Brett Weinstein was a  biology professor to Evergreen State  College in Oregon last year though a  campus mom targeted him when he refused  to leave campus because of the color of  his skin  you took a principled position as a  liberal by the way Weinstein later felt  he had to resign he did this week he  testified to Congress about free speech  on campus and after he did that he spoke  with us there it is professor thanks for  joining us  I took air thanks for inviting me back  on so well amazingly it's been a year  since all of this happened and we talked  to you right in the middle of it you've  had a lot of time to reflect on it what  are the lessons that you're taking from  the experience that you went through wow  it is hard to imagine that it's been a  year but it has been almost exactly a  year and much has changed I think one of  the things that's most important is that  the conversation is in a very different  place than it was last May we are now  able to discuss issues of identity  politics and though people are still  inflamed on both sides of that dialogue  we are able to have those discussions in  a way that was virtually impossible last  May it seems to me that there is  actually a recognition that the country  is in danger and it's being placed in  danger by identity politics both on the  far right and the far left and so I see  a movement led in part by the  intellectual dark web and the heterodox  Academy and fire towards what I would  call the patriotic center where people  who disagree over many issues of policy  find themselves aligned over basic  American principles yes so as I recall  you mean you were kind of caught unaware  by this you were absorbed in your  academic discipline you were kind of a  traditional free speech liberal you  didn't fully appreciate what had been  happening on campus until you stood up  and took the traditionally liberal  position that you shouldn't attack  people on the basis of their skin color  and you got pounded off campus and  threatened with violence  how have you reconciled to the Evergreen  itself have you spoken to the president  of the college that they apologized to  you where does that all stand now  while they have done quite the opposite  of apologizing they have in fact hired a  PR team that periodically demonizes my  wife and myself in the Olympian the  local paper and the paper of the state  capital and things on campus have  actually grown as much as it's hard to  imagine much worse than they were during  the riots of May 2017 so the Evergreen  State College has taken a turn towards  the authoritarian that is nothing if not  breathtaking people are being fired for  their willingness to offer any sort of  challenge to the administration those  who support the administration in its  reckless quest for what is falsely  called equity are being promoted and the  college faces a terrible financial  crisis as a result of the fact that  students are not showing up having seen  what the campus is descending into  people have decided to go elsewhere for  college which has put the college six  million dollars in the red it's also sad  and crazy so unless I'm missing  something all you did was say I'm not  gonna leave because I'm the wrong skin  color I mean took a kind of traditional  civil rights position I think on what  was going on at Evergreen on what  grounds are they attacking you what are  they saying about you well ironically  the challenge the one that appears to  resonate with people on the far left is  that my talking to you during the riots  brought violence from the alt-right to  campus which is I stretch to say at  least it's not that there weren't  threats to the campus there were there  was one threat in particular phoned in  from a man in New Jersey who claimed to  have a gun and be heading for campus he  did not have a gun and at any rate was  in New Jersey but but the the argument  effectively is that standing up and  speaking about what was taking place was  breaking ranks in a way that damaged the  college but what has never said is that  you invited me to talk to you and your  audience at a time  and the rest of the press was looking  the other way and what is hard for  people to appreciate is that at the time  that I was on your program the president  of the college George bridges had  literally stood down the police force so  that they were unable to intervene  between rioters and members of the  community like myself and the the  rioters took up carrying baseball bats  and batons they challenged people who  disagreed with them physically and so it  was quite a dangerous situation and the  idea that at a moment when as as I  understand it from the chief of police I  was apparently being hunted for car to  car by protesters I don't know what they  had in mind but at a time when that was  taking place in my own neighborhood  going on a program with an audience  understood to be right-of-center was  like some kind of sin ice unbelievable I  thought then and I think now I doubt  we've ever voted for the same candidate  but I really admired your bravery and I  admire it now Thank You professor and  Godspeed  we support you thank you
 

